  ],
  "text": "Sound is not a thing that travels. It is a squeeze moving through stuff. Nothing actually goes from the speaker to your ear. Each bit of air just shoves its neighbour. That shove passes along, and what arrives at your eardrum is a pattern of pressure. Which means sound needs something to shove. Take the stuff away and there is nothing to pass on. So space is silent. Completely, permanently silent. Every explosion you have ever heard in a film was invented in a studio. And the denser the stuff, the faster the shove gets handed on. In air, about three hundred and forty metres a second. In water, roughly four times that. In steel, about fifteen times. Put your ear to a rail and you hear the train early. Sound is not a message being carried. It is a shove being passed along. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
